solution_md:
Rewrite  $\pi^\pi$  as  $e^{\pi\log(\pi)}.$ First, we'll come up with an approximation for  $\log(\pi)$ . Using the remarkable fact that  $e^6 \approx \pi^5 + \pi^4$ , we have   $$ 6 \approx 4\log(\pi) + \log(1 + \pi) = 5\log(\pi) + \log(1 + \frac{1}{\pi}) \implies \log(\pi) \approx \frac{6 - \log(1 + \frac{1}{\pi})}{5}. $$   Then we want 
\begin{align*}
e^{\pi\log(\pi)} &\approx e^{\tfrac{6\pi}{5}}(1 + \frac{1}{\pi})^{\tfrac{-\pi}{5}} 
&\approx e^{3.77}e^{-\tfrac{1}{5}} 
&= e^{3.57}
\end{align*}
where we use the fact that  $(1 + \frac{1}{\pi})^{\pi} \approx e.$  Although this approximation is very shoddy, raising both sides to the  $\tfrac{1}{5}$  power significantly improves our estimation. Using the fact that  $\log(2) \approx 0.693$ , we have  $3.57 \approx 5\log(2) + .1$ . Then   $$ e^{3.57} \approx 32e^{.1} \approx 32(1 + .1 + .005) = 35.36 $$   using  $e^x \approx 1 + x + \tfrac{x^2}{2}$  for small  $x$ . Since all of our approximations are slight underapproximations, the most reasonable answer choice is  $36$ .

