This problem is a good test of how well you understand complex numbers/vectors. With good intuition, the problem dies in a few seconds.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>Consider the regular 18-gon below. Each exterior angle of it is  $20^{\circ}$ .
[asy]
unitsize(100);
pair[] a = {(0, 0)};
for (int i = 0; i < 18; ++i) {
a.push(a[a.length - 1] + dir(20*i + 20) - dir(20*i));
}
for (int i = 0; i < 18; ++i) {
draw(a[i]--a[i + 1], MidArrow);
}
for (int i = 0; i < 18; ++i) {
dot(a[i]);
}
[/asy]
It is comprised of 18 vectors. We wish to delete five of its vectors so that the remaining 13 vectors still form a polygon; i.e. have zero sum. To do that, the five vectors we delete must have zero sum as well.

An easy way to ensure that occurs is to delete two opposite vectors, and three vectors forming an equilateral triangle:
[asy]
unitsize(100);
pair[] a = {(0, 0)};
int[] c = {};
for (int i = 0; i < 18; ++i) {
a.push(a[a.length - 1] + dir(20*i + 20) - dir(20*i));
if (i == 4 || i == 13) c.push(1); else if (i == 2 || i == 8 || i == 14) c.push(2); else c.push(0);
}
for (int i = 0; i < 18; ++i) {
draw(a[i]--a[i + 1], c[i] == 0 ? black : (c[i] == 1 ? rgb(0.6,0.6,1) : rgb(1,0.6,0.6)), MidArrow);
}
for (int i = 0; i < 18; ++i) {
dot(a[i]);
}
[/asy]
This gives the following polygon:
[asy]
unitsize(100);
pair[] a = {(0, 0)};
int[] c = {}; int z = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < 18; ++i) {
if (i != 4 && i != 13 && i != 2 && i != 8 && i != 14) {
a.push(a[a.length - 1] + dir(20*i + 20) - dir(20*i));
c.push(z);
z = 0;
}
z += 20;
}
for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; ++i) {
draw(a[i]--a[i + 1]);
}
z = 360;
for (int i = 1; i < c.length; ++i) {
z -= c[i];
}
c[0] = z;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; ++i) {
dot(a[i]);
label(" $" + string(c[i]) + "^{\circ}$ ", a[i], dir(-a[(i + 1) % 13] - a[(i - 1) % 13] + 2 * a[i % 13]));
}
[/asy]</details>

This is really a combinatorial problem about complex numbers.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>One seeks a combination of 13 roots to  $X^{18}-1$  which have zero sum, and satisfy certain convexity conditions.

Shown below is one example, which turns out to be unique up to similarity.

[asy]size(12cm);
pair[] A = new pair[14];
real[] del = {0, 20, 40, 100, 120, 140, 160, 200, 220, 240, 280, 320, 340, 360};
pen[] colors = {blue, heavygreen, red, red, blue, grey, red, heavygreen, red, blue, red, grey, red};

// 13-gon
defaultpen(fontsize(8pt));
A[0] = (-0.4, -1.7);
// Define points
for (int i=1; i<=13; ++i) {
A[i] = A[i-1] + dir(del[i-1]);
}
// Fill region
fill(A[0]--A[1]--A[2]--A[3]--A[4]--A[5]--A[6]--A[7]--A[8]--A[9]--A[10]--A[11]--A[12]--cycle, opacity(0.2)+palecyan);
// Edges and labels
for (int i=1; i<=13; ++i) {
draw(A[i]--A[i-1], colors[i-1]);
label(" $"+(string)i+"$ ", A[i]--A[i-1], dir(del[i-1]-90), colors[i-1]+1);
}
// Do the dots afterwards
for (int i=1; i<=13; ++i) {
dot(" $+"+(string)(del[i]-del[i-1])+"^{\circ}$ ", A[i], 2.5*dir(-A[i]), fontsize(4pt));
}


// The wheel
transform t = shift((4.5,0))*scale(1.5);
filldraw(t*unitcircle, opacity(0.1)+lightcyan, heavycyan);
pen[] wheel_colors = {blue, heavygreen, red, orange, orange, red, blue, grey, red, orange, heavygreen, red, blue, orange, red, orange, grey, red};
