Firstly, we want to use Roots of Unity, so factor out a factor of  $2$ , and shift the index of  $k$  down by zero to get the following sum:

\begin{align*}
    \sum_{k=0}^{10} \frac{\sin(2^{k+4} \frac{2\pi}{89})}{\sin(2^k \frac{2\pi}{89})}
\end{align*}

Rewrite trigonometric terms as exponential terms instead; set  $\omega = e^{2i\pi/89}$ , and set  $\omega_k$  as  $(e^{2i\pi/89})^{2^k}$ . We then have:

\begin{align*}
    \sum_{k=0}^{10} \frac{\sin(2^{k+4} \frac{2\pi}{89})}{\sin(2^k \frac{2\pi}{89})} &= \sum_{k=0}^{10}\frac{\omega_{k}^{16} - \omega_{k}^{-16}}{\omega_{k}^1-\omega_{k}^{-1}} 
    &= \sum_{k=0}^{10} (\omega_k^{15} + \omega_k^{13} + \dots + \omega_k^{-13} + \omega_k^{-15})
\end{align*}

Notice how we have  $11 \cdot 16 = 176$  terms that are all some power of  $\omega$ . What would really be nice is if all  $176$  of these terms covered all  $88$  roots of unity  $\omega, \omega^2, \dots, \omega^{88}$  exactly twice; we will prove just that. 

Denote set  $S = \{ -15, -13, \dots, 13, 15 \}$ . Any two terms in our sum can be written as  $\omega^{s}_k$ , for some  $s \in S$  and  $k$ . Consider some primitive residue  $g$  modulo  $89$ . We can check via orders that  $g^8 \equiv 2 \pmod{89}$ , implying that for two powers  $x = 2^{k_1} \cdot s_1$  and  $y = 2^{k_2} \cdot s_2$ , where  $s_1 \in S$  and  $s_2 \in S$ , we must have  $\text{ord} s_1 \equiv \text{ord} s_2 \pmod{8}$ . This implies  $11 \text{ord} s_1 \equiv 11\text{ord} s_2 \pmod{88}$ . Checking the  $11$ -th powers of our residues in set  $S$  and verifying that each one is covered exactly  $2$  times finishes.

