The graph of  $f_{100}(x)$  is something of the following sort: 


- At  $x = 0$  we have  $f_{100}(0) = 50$ .
- $f$  is increasing with slope  $1$  over  $(0, 1)$ , decreasing over  $(1, 3)$ , increasing over  $(3, 6)$  and so on, until at  $x = \frac{100\cdot 101}{2}$  the graph remains increasing.

Thus our value of  $c$  is simply  $x = \frac{100 \cdot 101}{2}$ . Now to compute the area note that graph is symmetric about the y-axis so we may simply compute the area sandwiched between  $f_{100}(x)$  and the x-axis from  $\left(0, \frac{100 \cdot 101}{2} \right)$ .

We do this by summing over a bunch of trapezoids, namely in the intervals  $(0, 1)$ ,  $(1, 3)$ ,  $(3, 6)$  and so on. This yields,
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i = 1}^{100} \frac{\left[50 + \left(1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \dots + (-1)^{i}(i - 1) \right)\right] + \left[50 + \left(1 - 2 + 3 - 4 + \dots + (-1)^{i + 1}(i) \right)\right] }{2} \cdot i
\end{align*}
For odd  $i$  the inner sum evaluates to,
\begin{align*}
i \left( \frac{\left(50 - \frac{ i  - 1}{2} \right) + \left( 50 - \frac{ i - 1}{2} + i \right)}{2} \right) = \frac{101i}{2} 
\end{align*}
For even  $i$  the inner sum is,
\begin{align*}
i \left( \frac{\left(50 - \frac{i - 2}{2} + (i - 1) \right) + \left(50 - \frac{i}{2} \right)}{2}\right) = 50i
\end{align*}
Thus the sum is simply,
\begin{align*}
\frac{101}{2}\left(1 + 3 + \dots + 99 \right) + 50 \left(2 + 4 + \dots + 100 \right) = \frac{101}{2} \left(50 \cdot 51 - 50\right)+ 50(50 \cdot 51) = \frac{507500}{2}
\end{align*}
Now we double this to account for the second half of the graph, for a final answer of  $\boxed{507500}$ .

