\end{align*}
Hence the transformation
\[ \langle a, b, c, d \rangle \mapsto \tfrac12 \langle a+b+c+d, a+b-c-d, a-b+c-d, a-b-c+d \rangle \]
preserves squares, and the key detail is that now the first coordinate will always be  $10$ , so we can ignore it.

Now we only have a  $3$ -dimensional problem! The points  $\langle a, b, c, d \rangle$  with  $\{a, b, c, d\} = \{1, 2, 3, 4\}$  map to \[ \bigl\{\tfrac12(a+b-c-d), \tfrac12(a-b+c-d), \tfrac12(a-b-c+d)\bigr\} = \{0, \pm1, \pm2\}, \] where each  $\pm$  takes on exactly one choice.

In other words, <span style="color:blue">the  $24$  possible 3d points are given by the  $24$  possible 3-dimensional knight moves</span>.

Now if we fix the first coordinate to be  $-2$ ,  $-1$ ,  $0$ ,  $1$ ,  $2$ , we get  $1$ ,  $1$ ,  $2$ ,  $1$ ,  $1$  possible squares respectively, and by bashy casework there are no other types of possible squares.

So, there are  $3 \cdot 6 = \boxed{18}$  possible squares.</details>

