Define  $\{a_n\}$  and  $b$  as 
\[
\begin{cases}
    a_n &= \left( \left(  \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{ \left( 2^n \right)} - \left(  \frac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{ \left( 2^n \right)} \right)^2 = \left(  \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{ \left( 2^{n+1} \right)} + \left(  \frac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{ \left( 2^{n+1} \right)} - 2 
    b &= \frac{2023}{5}.
\end{cases}
\]
We wish to evaluate
\[ \sqrt{ba_1 + \sqrt{ba_2 + \sqrt{ba_3 + \sqrt{\dots} }}}.\]

The main claim is that for any positive integer  $n$ ,
\[ \sqrt{ba_n + \sqrt{ba_{n+1} + \sqrt{ba_{n+2} + \sqrt{\dots}}}} = \left( \frac{1+\sqrt{1+4b}}{2} \right) \left( \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{2^n} + \left( \frac{1-\sqrt{1+4b}}{2} \right) \left( \frac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{2^n} \]
There is probably a normal, non-terrible way to prove this, but I am high, **so we will use induction: our base case starts with  $n = \infty$ , and our inductive step is  $n \implies n-1$ .** **cue applause**
[rule]**Base case:** If  $n = \infty$  (well, not exactly infinity, but just some very large number), it follows that
\[a_k \approx \left(  \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{ \left( 2^{k+1} \right)}\]
for  $k \geq n$ . Note that this approximation is off by about  $2$  - go cry about it. Now, letting  $\alpha = \left(  \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{ \left( 2^{n+1} \right)}$ , it follows that
\begin{align*}
    & \sqrt{ba_n + \sqrt{ba_{n+1} + \sqrt{ba_{n+2} + \sqrt{\dots}}}} 
    \approx ~ & \sqrt{b \alpha + \sqrt{b \alpha ^2 + \sqrt{b \alpha^4 + \sqrt{\dots}}}} 
    = ~ & \sqrt{\alpha} \cdot \sqrt{b + \sqrt{b + \sqrt{b + \sqrt{\dots}}}} 
    = ~ & \left( \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{ \left( 2^{n} \right)} \cdot \frac{1+\sqrt{1+4b}}{2} 
    \approx ~ & \left( \frac{1+\sqrt{1+4b}}{2} \right) \left( \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{2^n} + \left( \frac{1-\sqrt{1+4b}}{2} \right) \left( \frac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{2^n}.
\end{align*}
Base case done.**Inductive step:** I'm too lazy to write this one out, but figure it out yourself if you want - assume  $n$ , prove  $n-1$ . Miraculously things cancel out and turn out really nice, so I'm probably missing some nicer solution.
[rule]
Now, we extract: our final answer is
\begin{align*}
    & \left( \frac{1+\sqrt{1+4b}}{2} \right) \left( \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{2^1} + \left( \frac{1-\sqrt{1+4b}}{2} \right) \left( \frac{1-\sqrt{5}}{2} \right)^{2^1} 
    = ~ & \left( \frac{1+\sqrt{1+4b}}{2} \right) \left( \frac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2} \right) + \left( \frac{1-\sqrt{1+4b}}{2} \right) \left( \frac{3-\sqrt{5}}{2} \right) 
    = ~ & \frac{3 + \sqrt{8097}}{2}.
\end{align*}
The final answer is  $3 + 8097 + 2 = \boxed{8102}$ .

