Proof: We will show that  $\triangle KDS\sim \triangle CDN$ . Clearly  $\angle KDS=\angle CDN=90^{\circ}-\frac{\angle C}{2}$  and  $\frac{DN}{CD}=\sin \frac{\angle C}{2}$ . Denote the inradius by  $r$  again. Observe that 
\begin{align*}
DK&=BK-BD
&=CD-BD
&=r\left(\frac{1}{\tan \frac{\angle C}{2}}-\frac{1}{\tan\frac{\angle B}{2}}\right)
&=r\left(\frac{\cos\frac{\angle C}{2}}{\sin\frac{\angle C}{2}}-\frac{\cos \frac{\angle B}{2}}{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}}\right)
&=r\left(\frac{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}\cos\frac{\angle C}{2}-\cos \frac{\angle B}{2}\sin\frac{\angle C}{2}}{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}\sin\frac{\angle C}{2}}\right)
&=r\left(\frac{\sin\left(\frac{\angle B}{2}-\frac{\angle C}{2}\right)}{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}\sin\frac{\angle C}{2}}\right)
&=r\left(\frac{\sin\frac{\angle B-\angle C}{2}}{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}\sin\frac{\angle C}{2}}\right)
\end{align*}
In addition, from Law of Sines on  $\triangle IDS,$ \begin{align*}
\frac{DS}{ID}&=\frac{\sin \angle SID}{\sin\angle DSI}
\frac{DS}{r}&=\frac{\sin \angle LID}{\sin \left(180^{\circ}-\angle ESA\right)}
&=\frac{\sin\left(90^{\circ}-\angle DLI\right)}{\sin\left(\angle SAE+\angle AES\right)}
&=\frac{\sin\left(\angle ALC-90^{\circ}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{\angle A}{2}+90^{\circ}+\angle IED\right)}
&=\frac{\sin\left(180^{\circ}-\angle LCA-\angle CAL-90^{\circ}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{\angle A}{2}+90^{\circ}+\frac{\angle C}{2}\right)}
&=\frac{\sin\left(90^{\circ}-\angle C-\frac{\angle A}{2}\right)}{\sin\left(180^{\circ}-\frac{\angle B}{2}\right)}
&=\frac{\sin\left(\frac{\angle A+\angle B+\angle C}{2}-\angle C-\frac{\angle A}{2}\right)}{\sin \frac{\angle B}{2}}
\frac{DS}{r}&=\frac{\sin\left(\frac{\angle B-\angle C}{2}\right)}{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}}
DS&=r\left(\frac{\sin\left(\frac{\angle B-\angle C}{2}\right)}{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}}\right).
\end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align*}
\frac{DS}{KD}&=\frac{r\left(\frac{\sin\left(\frac{\angle B-\angle C}{2}\right)}{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}}\right)}{r\left(\frac{\sin\frac{\angle B-\angle C}{2}}{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}\sin\frac{\angle C}{2}}\right)}
&=\frac{\sin\left(\frac{\angle B-\angle C}{2}\right)}{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}}\cdot \frac{\sin\frac{\angle B}{2}\sin\frac{\angle C}{2}}{\sin\left(\frac{\angle B-\angle C}{2}\right)}
&=\sin\frac{\angle C}{2}
&=\frac{DN}{CD}.
\end{align*}
Since we also know that  $\angle KDS=\angle CDN,$  by SAS similarity,  $\triangle KDS\sim\triangle CDN$ . Thus,  $\angle DSK=\angle DNC=90^{\circ},$  so  $KS$  is perpendicular to  $DE$  as desired. A very similar argument finds that  $KT$  is perpendicular to  $FD$ . $\phantom{Finally done}$  
