Let  $E(x)$  denote Andy's expected score when Andy has  $x$  points. The given condition is equivalent to \begin{align*}
E(x+1) &= \dfrac 12 \cdot (-E(x)) + \dfrac 12 \cdot (E(x)+1) 
&= \dfrac 12
\end{align*}
so Andy's expected score is  $\fbox{0.5}$  points no matter how many points he begins with.

We split up the rectangle into a right triangle along one of the diagonals. Note the probability still stays the same for this triangle. It is also easy to see that a point lies closer to a side if it lies on the correct side of the angle bisector.

The two triangles with the desired region have its base on a side of the rectangle and its height the inradius of the larger triangle (formed by side and diagonals). Let the sides have lengths  $a$  and  $b$ .

Each triangle formed by the diagonals has area  $\frac{ab}{4}$ . The two triangles have semiperimeter  $3 + \frac a2$  and  $3 + \frac b2$  respectively. Thus their inradius is  $\frac{ab}{12 + 2a}$  and  $\frac{ab}{12 + 2b}$  respectively.

We would like 

\begin{align*}
    \frac{\frac{a^2b}{12 + 2a} + \frac{ab^2}{12 + 2b}}{ab} = \frac{19}{49} &\implies \frac{a}{12 + 2a} + \frac{b}{12 + 2b} = \frac{19}{49} 
    &\implies 49(12a + 4ab + 12b) = 19(24a + 4ab + 24b + 144) 
    &\implies 11a + 10ab + 11b = 228.
\end{align*}

Also,  $a^2 + b^2 = 36$ .

\begin{align*}
    a^2 + b^2 = 36 &\implies 5a^2 + 5b^2 = 180 
    &\implies 5a^2 + 10ab + 5b^2 + 11a + 11b = 408 
    &\implies 5(a+b)^2 + 11(a+b) = 408.
\end{align*}

Solving this quadratic yields  $a + b = 8$ . This in turn yields  $ab = \boxed{14}$ .

