For a general graph  $G$  with vertex set  $V$ , consider the space  $R^{|V|}$ . For each edge  $ij$  associate it with the hyperplane  $x_i = x_j$ . For a directed edge  $i \rightarrow j$  associate it with the inequality  $x_i > x_j$ . Then note that any acyclic orientation results in a consistent set of inequalities, which describe a region (note that this also holds in reverse, so the associated set of inequalities for any region also results in an acyclic orientation). So to count the number of acyclic orientations it suffices to count the number of regions divided by those hyperplanes. Recall that for any hyperplane arrangement  $\mathcal{A}$  we can define the *characteristic polynomial*  $\chi_{\mathcal{A}}$ . It is well-known that for the specific case of hyperplane arrangement determined by a graph  $G$ , the characteristic polynomial is equal to the chromatic polynomial  $\chi_G$  where  $\chi_G (p)$  counts the number of vertex colorings of  $G$  with  $p$  colors (e.g. take the hyperplane arrangement in  $F_p$ , because the hyperplanes are already in general position). Now, if  $r(\mathcal{A})$  is the number of regions divided by  $\mathcal{A}$ , then note that  $r$  satisfies the *deletion-restriction recursion*  $r(\mathcal{A}) = r(\mathcal{A}') + r(\mathcal{A''})$  where  $\mathcal{A}'$  is  $\mathcal{A}$  minus one of its hyperplanes, and  $\mathcal{A}''$  is the intersection of  $\mathcal{A}'$  with  $H$ . Note that  $\chi_{\mathcal{A}}$  also satisfies a deletion-restriction recursion  $\chi_{\mathcal{A}} = \chi_{\mathcal{A}'} - \chi_{\mathcal{A}''}$ , so in particular  $(-1)^n \chi_{\mathcal{A}}$  satisfies the same recursion if  $n$  denotes the dimension of the space. We can check that plugging in  $-1$  gives the correct base cases, so  $r(\mathcal{A}) = (-1)^n \chi_{\mathcal{A}} (-1)$ . For our case  $n = 7075$ . To find the chromatic polynomial, we can do polynomial recursion, splitting each row by  $aba$  or  $abc$  coloring. Thus, the chromatic polynomial  $\chi(p)$  is the sum of the components of  $\[
\begin{pmatrix}
(p-2)^2 + p-1 & (p-3)^2 + 2(p-2) 
(p-2)^2(p-3) + (p-1)(p-2) & 2(p-2)^2 + (p-3)((p-3)(p-4) + 2(p-3)+1)
\end{pmatrix}^{2024}
\begin{pmatrix}
p(p-1) 
p(p-1)(p-2)
\end{pmatrix}
\]$ (the top component denoting  $aba$  and bottom component denoting  $abc$ ). The rest is just plugging in  $-1$  and doing standard computation.

