Let  $k$  be the number of herds in total, and let  $a_1, a_2,\dots, a_k$  be the lengths of the herds. Then, there are  $a_1a_2\cdots a_k$  ways to choose the herd leaders, and the only condition on the  $a_i$  is that  $a_1 + 1 + a_2 + 1\dots + 1 + a_k = 100 $ , or equivalently,  $\sum_i a_i = 101 - k$ . Hence, the answer is
\begin{align*}
N &= \sum_{k\in \mathbb{N}}\sum_{a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_k = 101-k}\prod_{i = 1}^k a_i
&= \sum_{k\in \mathbb{N}}\left([x^{101-k}]\left(\sum_{j = 0}^\infty jx^j\right)^k\right)
&= \sum_{k\in \mathbb{N}}\left([x^{101-k}] \left(x\cdot D_x\left[\sum_{j = 0}^\infty x^j\right]\right)^k\right)
&= \sum_{k\in \mathbb{N}}\left([x^{101-k}] \left(x\cdot D_x\left[(1-x)^{-1}\right]\right)^k\right)
&= \sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\left([x^{101-k}]\left(\frac{x}{(1-x)^2}\right)^k\right)
&= \sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\left([x^{101-k}]\frac{x^k}{(1-x)^{2k}}\right)
&= \sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\left([x^{101-k}]\left(\sum_{j = 0}^\infty \binom{2k-1+j}{2k-1}x^{j+k}\right)\right)
&= \sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\binom{2k-1 + (101-2k)}{2k-1}
&= \sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\binom{100}{2k-1}
&= \frac{\left(\sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\binom{100}{2k-1}+\sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\binom{100}{2k}\right)+\left(\sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\binom{100}{2k-1} - \sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}\binom{100}{2k}\right)}{2}
&= \frac{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}1^{100-k}1^k\binom{100}{k} - \sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}}(-1)^{100-k}1^k\binom{100}{k}}{2}
&= \frac{(1+1)^{100}- (-1 + 1)^{100}}{2}
&= \frac{2^{100}}{2}
&= 2^{50}
&= 1024^5
&\equiv 24^5\pmod{1000}
&\equiv \boxed{624}\pmod{1000}
\end{align*}

Reframe the unwieldy equality chain as a symmetric system of equations:
\begin{align*}
x^2-xy+yz-zx&=1
y^2-xy-yz+zx&=2
z^2+xy-yz-zx&=c.
\end{align*}
On one hand, summing up all the equations together gives
\begin{align*}
x^2+y^2+z^2-xy-yz-zx&=c+3
\implies(x-y)^2+(y-z)^2+(z-x)^2&=2c+6.~(\spadesuit)
\end{align*}
On the other hand, each equation factors as follows:
\begin{align*}
(z-x)(x-y)&=-1
(x-y)(y-z)&=-2
(y-z)(z-x)&=-c.
\end{align*}
These solve as  $(x-y,y-z,z-x)=\pm(\tfrac{\sqrt{-2c}}{c},\tfrac{\sqrt{-2c}}{1},\tfrac{\sqrt{-2c}}{2})$ , and plugging into  $(\spadesuit)$  produces
\[(\tfrac{\sqrt{-2c}}{c})^2+(\tfrac{\sqrt{-2c}}{1})^2+(\tfrac{\sqrt{-2c}}{2})^2=2c+6,\]
yielding  $c^2=\tfrac{4}{9}$ .
